 "standard_url": "https://tea.texas.gov/about-tea/laws-and-rules/sboe-rules-tac/sboe-tac-currently-in-effect/ch111c.pdf",
 "standard_text": "A.10A add and subtract polynomials of degree one and degree two; A.10B multiply polynomials of degree one and degree two; A.10D rewrite polynomial expressions of degree one and degree two in equivalent forms using the distributive property",
 "meta_desc": "Free Algebra I polynomial worksheets for Texas TEKS A.10A, A.10B and A.10D: degree and standard form, add, subtract and multiply polynomials, special products and applications. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Combine like terms; distribute every term.",
 "key_sub": "(2x + 3)(x − 4) = 2x² − 5x − 12",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Add or subtract <b>like terms</b> only: same variable, same exponent.</li><li>When subtracting, change the sign of <b>every</b> term in the second polynomial.</li><li>To multiply, use the distributive property: each term times each term.</li><li>(<i>a</i> + <i>b</i>)<sup>2</sup> = <i>a</i><sup>2</sup> + 2<i>ab</i> + <i>b</i><sup>2</sup>, and (<i>a</i> + <i>b</i>)(<i>a</i> − <i>b</i>) = <i>a</i><sup>2</sup> − <i>b</i><sup>2</sup>.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>(2<i>x</i> + 3)(<i>x</i> − 4)</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 8<i>x</i> + 3<i>x</i> − 12</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 2<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 5<i>x</i> − 12</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Not distributing the minus sign</td><td>−(3<i>x</i> − 2) = −3<i>x</i> + 2.</td></tr><tr><td>Adding exponents when adding</td><td><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> = 2<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>.</td></tr><tr><td>Forgetting the middle term</td><td>(<i>x</i> + 3)<sup>2</sup> has 6<i>x</i>.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
